---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const posts = (await getCollection('writing', ({ data }) => !data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf());
const recent = posts.slice(0, 6);
const fmt = (d) => d.toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' });
---
<Base>
  <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:2.6rem">
    I get curious about things and then read too much about them. This is where
    that ends up — mostly physics and infrastructure at the moment, but the
    subject drifts.
  </p>

  <p class="section-label">Writing</p>
  {posts.length === 0 && (
    <p style="color:var(--fg-muted)">Nothing published yet — first piece is in progress.</p>
  )}
  <ul class="entry-list">
    {recent.map(p => (
      <li class="entry">
        <a class="entry-title" href={p.data.url ?? `/writing/${p.id}/`}>{p.data.title}</a>
        <div class="entry-date">
          {fmt(p.data.date)}{p.data.series ? ` · ${p.data.series}` : ''}
        </div>
        {p.data.description && <p>{p.data.description}</p>}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  {posts.length > recent.length && (
    <p style="margin-top:1.6rem"><a href="/writing/">All writing →</a></p>
  )}

  <p class="section-label" style="margin-top:2.6rem">Built</p>
  <ul class="entry-list">
    <li class="entry">
      <a class="entry-title" href="/datacenters/">Data Center Power Atlas</a>
      <div class="entry-date">Updated daily</div>
      <p>
        Where the world's data centers are, how much electricity they draw, and
        what that does to emissions. Every parameter is fitted to a national
        statistics office that measured the thing, rather than to my judgement —
        and the page says which sources it refuses to use, and why.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="entry">
      <a class="entry-title" href="/units/">Every Unit Still in Use</a>
      <div class="entry-date">342 units, 30 domains</div>
      <p>
        The units people actually write down today, each tagged by where it came
        from: a body part, a trade practice, a measurement of the natural world,
        a physical constant, or a committee decision. Includes who chose it and
        what it displaced, and says so plainly where the record is simply lost.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="entry">
      <a class="entry-title" href="/symbols/">Reading the Equation</a>
      <div class="entry-date">222 symbols</div>
      <p>
        Where each mathematical and physical symbol came from, who chose the
        glyph, and the other three things it means in somebody else's field.
        Sigma is a standard deviation, a stress, a conductivity, a surface
        tension and a Pauli matrix, and nobody is confused until they change
        rooms.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
